From cc3fa5615a43a550d023a6158abe190870067a19 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 1aeo <github@1aeo.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 05:28:25 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 18/20] rephist: Count CREATE and BEGIN_DIR cells per source
 and minute

The network team asks whether a flood of circuits comes from many
sources or from a few: 1,000 circuits a second from 1,000 addresses
calls for a different fix than from ten. The CREATE counters by previous
hop say how many circuits arrive over each kind of channel, not how they
spread across the peers sending them.

Count each CREATE cell, and each BEGIN_DIR cell, by previous hop and by
its place among the cells of the same kind that its source sent in the
same minute, in the bands of tor_relay_begindir_cell_total (the 1st, the
2nd to the 10th, the 11th to the 100th, the 101st to the 1000th, or
later), as tor_relay_create_cell_source_total and
tor_relay_begindir_cell_source_total. The first band counts the sources
active in each minute; the later bands are what a limit per source would
cut.

A source is what tor would limit: all client channels from one address,
while the DoS subsystem tracks it (whenever one of its defenses is on,
from the address's last new connection until tor forgets it a day or two
later), or each channel from a relay. A client channel from an address
tor doesn't track is its own source. Each source keeps its minute and
two counts that stop at the last band, 8 bytes on the channel and on the
client address entry, in a source_minute_t of their own header so that
channel.h needn't include dos.h. Bridges don't count these, so as to say
nothing about their users. Only the MetricsPort shows these counts, so a
relay without one skips them, and with them the address lookup each cell
from a client channel costs.

Part of #41339.
